          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "निकलना (nikalnā) — \"to set out, to leave\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What you say when you are on the point of walking out of a door. It is the going-out kind of action, where the thing leaves under its own steam, and it has a partner, nikālnā, with a long ā, which means to take something out."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Lengthen the vowel and the doer changes: that pairing is one of Hindi's tidiest machines, and it runs through dozens of everyday words. Sanskrit niṣkal-, 'to go forth', stands behind both."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four, and this one is something you do."
